**Theorem 28.1 (exact right-ray singleton and worst target).** For every
$D\ge n\ge1$,

$$
M_D(\{R^n\})=m(n),\qquad
\max_{p\in\mathcal P_D}M_D(\{p\})=m(D).
\tag{GDB.111}
$$

The lower bound allows arbitrary correlated code fibers, all original
variable shapes, arbitrary finite off-target original-root queries and
zero-Read branches.

Proof. On the $2n+1$ actual witnesses of Lemma 26.3 for $p=R^n$,
(GDB.107) has first-branch count

$$
b(B)=0,\qquad b(j,0)=2(n-j)+2,\qquad b(j,1)=2(n-j)+1.
\tag{GDB.112}
$$

These are exactly the integers $0,1,\ldots,2n$, without repetition.
The witnesses therefore have distinct full trajectories: the first
branch count is already recovered from a trajectory. This uses all
subsequent authentic counts, rather than only the initial reply.

Fix any lawful competitor for this singleton task and one attained
complete export value $e$. At $e,D,p$ its deterministic decision to
make zero Reads, or its entire query word, is fixed before a reply
and every count. In the query case there are at most four replies,
regardless of the length or location of that word. Equal replies at
this same complete name produce identical final predictors and hence
the same full decoded trajectory. At most four of these pairwise
distinct witness trajectories can therefore have name $e$. A zero-Read
branch accommodates at most one. Arbitrary post-request computation
or predictor storage cannot distinguish identical inputs.

The $2n+1$ witnesses all belong to the mandatory original family.
Thus every complete export has at least
$\lceil(2n+1)/4\rceil=m(n)$ attained values. No prefix-query
normalization or projection of a correlated fiber was used in this
count. Theorem 27.2 supplies the matching upper. For an arbitrary
$p$ of length $n\le D$, that same upper is at most $m(D)$.
Taking $p=R^D$ proves the worst-target equality. $\square$

This is an exact maximum over singleton tasks, not a formula asserted
for every individual word. The proof permits the real descriptor
collisions in Convention 26.4.

